Event Plane

In this section, we introduce the Fourier expansion of azimuthal particle distribution and its properties with respect to the reaction plane. And we also introduce event plane which is the estimate of the true reaction plane determined by using the signal of flow itself.

#### Fourier Expansion of Azimuthal Distribution

Since the azimuthal distribution of emitted particles \(dN/d\phi\) is the periodic function with \(2\pi\) fundamental period, it is natural to expand azimuthal distribution into fourier series with \(2\pi\) period.

\[\begin{split}\frac{dN}{d\phi}&=\frac{x_{0}}{2\pi}+ \frac{1}{\pi}\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}(x_{n}\cos(n\phi)+y_{n}\sin(n\phi))\\ &=\frac{x_{0}}{2\pi}(1+2\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}(\frac{x_{n}}{x_{0}} \cos(n\phi)+\frac{y_{n}}{y_{0}}\sin(n\phi)))\end{split} \tag{3.1}\]

Because there is only a finite number of particles in each event, the Fourier coefficients \(x_{n}\) and \(y_{n}\) can be expressed as:

\[x_{n}=\int_{0}^{2\pi}d\phi\frac{dN}{d\phi}\cos(n\phi)=\sum_{n=1}^{M}w_{i}\cos (n\phi_{i})\equiv Q_{x} \tag{3.2}\]

\[y_{n}=\int_{0}^{2\pi}d\phi\frac{dN}{d\phi}\sin(n\phi)=\sum_{n=1}^{M}w_{i}\sin (n\phi_{i})\equiv Q_{y} \tag{3.3}\]

where i runs over all particles (\(M\)) used to determined the event plane, \(\phi_{i}\) is the azaimuthal angle of the emitted \(i^{\text{th}}\) particle and \(w_{i}\) is the weight (\(p_{T}\), \(\phi\) etc) to minimize the dispersion of event plane (i.e. maximum event plane resolution). We define the following two-dimentional vector \(\mathbf{Q}=(Q_{x},\;Q_{y})\) called as a flow vector.

If we assume \(\phi\) in Eq. 3.1 is defined relative to the reaction plane, then \(dN/d\phi\) becomes an even function and we can omit \(y_{n}\) terms since the integration would be zero in Eq. 3.3,

