#### Event Plane Resolution

According to Equation 3.8, the measured \(v_{2}\) is equal to the value of \(v_{2}^{\rm obs}\) divided by \(\langle\cos(n\Delta\Psi)\rangle\). This can be understood as following: when we measure the reaction plane, due to finite flow particles and flow signal, there should be some uncertainties. It causes the difference between the measured reaction plane (event plane) and the real reaction plane. Thus, the observed \(v_{2}\) should be corrected by the factor in Equation 3.8. [25]:

\[v_{2}=\frac{v_{2}^{\rm obs}}{\langle\cos[2(\Psi_{2}-\Psi_{r})]\rangle} \tag{3.13}\]

The brackets in the denominator means the average over the all events. \(v_{2}\), \(v_{2}^{\rm obs}\) represent the real elliptic flow and the observed elliptic flow signal; while \(\Psi_{2}\) and \(\Psi_{r}\) represent the measured azimuthal angle of reaction plane (event plane angle) and the azimuthal angle of real reaction plane. The denominator in the right side of the Equation, \(\langle\cos[2(\Psi_{2}-\Psi_{r})]\rangle\) is the event plane resolution. As it is not measurable, we usually calculate the event plane resolution for sub events firstly. The steps are following: At first, a full event is divided in to two equal sub events; then one can measure the event plane angle in two sub events; The resolution for the sub event is defined by Equation 3.14 [26].

\[\langle\cos[2(\Psi_{2}^{A}-\Psi_{r})]\rangle=\sqrt{\langle\cos[2(\Psi_{2}^{A}- \Psi_{2}^{B})]\rangle} \tag{3.14}\]

Since we have two independent event plane from west and east FTPC (or the random sub events from TPC), we can estimate the event plane resolution by measuring the relative azimuthal angle \(\Delta\Psi_{2}^{\rm FTPC}\equiv 2(\Psi_{2}^{\rm West}-\Psi_{2}^{\rm East})\). This is based on the assumption that there are no other correlations except flow effects. Taking into account that the multiplicity of the full event is twice as large as that of the sub-event, the full event plane resolution is given by Equation 3.15.

\[\langle\cos[2(\Psi_{2}-\Psi_{r})]\rangle\ =\ C\sqrt{\langle\cos[2(\Psi_{2}^{A}- \Psi_{2}^{B})]\rangle} \tag{3.15}\]

In the case of low resolution (\(\leq 0.2\)), such as for the FTPC event plane, \(C\) approaches \(\sqrt{2}\).

