### 3.4 \(v_{2}\) Methods

In this section, we discuss the methods to extract \(v_{2}\).

#### The Event Plane Method

Fig. 3.8 shows the \(dN/d(\phi-\Psi)\) distribution for \(K^{0}_{S}\) at a chosen \(p_{T}\) bin (0.8 - 1.0GeV/\(c\)). The measured \(v_{2}\) is extracted by fitting \(dN/d(\phi-\Psi)\) distribution with Fourier expansion of azimuthal distribution:

\[\frac{dN}{d(\phi-\Psi)}=N(1+2v_{2}^{\rm obs}\cos(2(\phi-\Psi_{2}))) \tag{3.16}\]

Where \(N\) and \(v_{2}^{\rm obs}\) are free parameters. The measured \(v_{2}\), i.e. \(v_{2}^{\rm obs}\), need to be corrected with event plane resolution by Eq. 3.13.

#### The Scalar Product Method

The Scalar Product method [1, 1] is similar to the Event Plane method, and gives \(v_{2}\) as:

\[v_{2}(p_{T}) =\frac{\langle Q_{2}u_{2,i}^{*}(p_{T})\rangle}{2\sqrt{\langle Q_{2 }^{4}Q_{2}^{8*}\rangle}} \tag{3.17}\]

where \(u_{2,i}=\cos(2\phi_{i})+i\sin(2\phi_{i})\) is a unit vector of the \(i\)th particle, \(Q_{2}=\sum_{k}u_{2,k}\) is the flow vector with the sum running over all other particles \(k\) in the event. The superscript * denotes the complex conjugate of a complex number. \(A\) and \(B\) denote the two subevents. In the case that \(Q_{2}\) is normalized to a unit vector, Eq. (3.17) reduces to the Event Plane method. In the Scalar Product method, one can use a different (re-centering) technique [15] to correct for detector effects, which presents an alternative to the weighting and shifting procedures. The Scalar Product method is applied to the \(v_{2}\) measurement of charged hadrons.

#### The \(v_{2}\) versus \(m_{\rm inv}\) Method

When we measure \(v_{2}\) of the reconstructed particles, such as \(K_{S}^{0}\), \(\Lambda\), \(\Xi\) and \(\Omega\), \(v_{2}\) versus \(m_{\rm inv}\) method is often use. In Fig. 3.5, it shows the invariant mass distributions for these particles. Even with the optimized topology cuts, there are still some residual combinatorial backgrounds. The \(v_{2}\) versus \(m_{\rm inv}\) method is designed for extracting the \(v_{2}\) of real signal.

